Everything in this repository — the AIDLC curriculum and lecture series, the
guides, the templates, the harness and its gates, the setup scripts, the
worked examples, and the proposal scaffold — is the original course work of
Kyoung Shin, created for CPSC 490 Undergraduate Seminar in Computer Science.

ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. No licence is granted by publication. Making this
repository publicly readable is for the convenience of enrolled students and
for colleagues evaluating the approach; it does not place the work in the
public domain and does not grant permission to reuse it.


PERMITTED WITHOUT ASKING

  1. Students currently enrolled in CPSC 490 or CPSC 491 with the author may
     copy, run, modify and build upon this material as required for their
     coursework, including copying the scaffold into their own team
     repository. This permission is for coursework only and does not extend
     to publication or redistribution outside the course.

  2. Anyone may read this repository, and may quote brief excerpts with
     attribution for the purposes of review, commentary, or scholarly
     citation.


REQUIRES WRITTEN PERMISSION — please ask first

  Any other use, including but not limited to:

  - adopting or adapting these materials for another course, workshop,
    seminar, or training programme (whether or not money changes hands);
  - redistributing, republishing, or mirroring any part of this repository;
  - including any part of it in a product, service, publication, textbook,
    or commercial training offering;
  - using it to train or fine-tune machine-learning models.

  Write to kshin@fullerton.edu. Requests from fellow instructors are welcome
  and normally granted — the author simply wishes to know who is using the
  material and to keep the record of authorship straight.


CITATION

  When permission has been granted, cite as:

      Shin, K. (2026). CPSC 490 AIDLC course scaffold: human-in-the-loop
      AI-driven development for undergraduate capstone projects.
      Department of Computer Science, California State University,
      Fullerton. Used with permission.


